ultramodern: hold the recompiled code to the console's rate

The counterpart to 0014, which made the recompiled code say how much of the
console's processor it stood for. This spends it.

`set_cpu_instruction_rate` gives the runtime a rate in instructions a second;
`recomp_cycle_spend` is called when that many have gone by and sleeps the thread
until the wall clock has caught up with the console's. Holding the thread is
right rather than merely convenient: the console has one processor, and while it
is working nothing else in the game runs either, which is exactly what a game
thread parked here does. The runtime's own threads -- the video interface, the
graphics thread, audio -- carry on, because they are the parts that are not the
processor.

Time is spent, not saved. When the wall clock has run ahead, because the game
was waiting on something rather than working, the credit is dropped and the
count re-based on the present; otherwise a game that idled for a second would be
allowed to run a second's worth of code at once afterwards.

The rate itself is not here, because it is a measurement and not a
specification: what an R4300 retires in a second is a fact about a processor
waiting on memory. `n64b-run` sets it, and PLAN.md has how it was arrived at and
what it does to Banjo-Tooie's first two and a half minutes.

--- a/ultramodern/include/ultramodern/ultramodern.hpp
+++ b/ultramodern/include/ultramodern/ultramodern.hpp
@@ -111,6 +111,15 @@
